# ---- Reelhouse environment ----
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in values as you enable features.
# Most values here are READ ON THE SERVER ONLY — never exposed to the browser.
# The few prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ are intentionally sent to the browser and are
# safe to publish (e.g. the Supabase anon key, which is protected by row-level
# security). The app runs fully on built-in mock data + localStorage if these
# are left blank.

# TMDB metadata (https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api).
# Provide either a v3 API key OR a v4 read access token.
# Leave blank to use the built-in mock catalog.
TMDB_API_KEY=

# Optional: base URL for TMDB images (rarely needs changing).
# TMDB_IMAGE_BASE=https://image.tmdb.org/t/p

# Optional: TMDB API host. Defaults to https://api.themoviedb.org/3. Some ISPs
# (notably in India) block that hostname; if live data times out, switch to
# TMDB's identical alias, which serves the same API:
# TMDB_API_BASE=https://api.tmdb.org/3

# ---- OpenSubtitles (in-player "Search subtitles") ----
# Powers Player Settings → Subtitles → Search subtitles. SERVER ONLY — the API
# key, username, and password are never sent to the browser (no NEXT_PUBLIC_).
# Leave blank to keep the search UI showing a "not configured" setup message.
#
# Setup (all free):
#   1. Create an account at https://www.opensubtitles.com/en/newuser
#   2. Register an API consumer at https://www.opensubtitles.com/en/consumers
#      → this gives you the API key (used for search AND download).
#   3. Your account username + password are additionally required to DOWNLOAD a
#      selected subtitle (login → bearer token). Downloads count against a daily
#      per-account quota (free tier is small; VIP raises it).
#
# API key alone → search works (results are listed, "Use" is disabled).
# API key + username + password → selecting a result loads it into the player.
# OPENSUBTITLES_API_KEY=
# OPENSUBTITLES_USERNAME=
# OPENSUBTITLES_PASSWORD=
# Optional User-Agent identifying this app to OpenSubtitles (defaults to
# "Reelhouse v1.0"). Some accounts register a specific app name/version.
# OPENSUBTITLES_APP_NAME=Reelhouse v1.0

# Authorized video provider (legacy variables, kept for compatibility). Leave
# blank: playback falls back to Reelhouse's own built-in player, which serves a
# clearly-labeled, freely-licensed bundled clip. NEVER point this at unauthorized
# streams, scrapers, or DRM-circumvention endpoints.
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_BASE=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_KEY=

# ---- Video provider slots 1–5 (all OFF until you connect a licensed provider) ----
# Five generic slots. Every one is unconfigured and disabled below, which is the
# state this app ships in: the provider picker is not rendered at all, playback
# uses Reelhouse's own built-in player, and nothing about that changes until you
# fill a slot in.
#
# Fill a slot in ONLY from the provider's own official documentation, and only for
# a provider you are licensed to embed. Do not copy a URL pattern from a third
# party, a forum, or another site — see docs/video-provider-setup.md, which is the
# full step-by-step guide (what each variable does, how to test, how to
# troubleshoot). Quick reference for slot N (1..5):
#
#   VIDEO_PROVIDER_N_NAME              Display name shown in the provider picker.
#                                      Default "Provider N".
#   VIDEO_PROVIDER_N_ENABLED           1|true|yes|on to make the slot selectable.
#                                      Default off.
#   VIDEO_PROVIDER_N_PRIORITY          Integer, LOWER is preferred. Default N*10,
#                                      so any configured slot automatically takes
#                                      over from the built-in player (10000).
#   VIDEO_PROVIDER_N_MOVIE_URL         Movie player URL template. Tokens: {id}
#                                      (the TMDB id).
#   VIDEO_PROVIDER_N_TV_URL            TV player URL template. Tokens: {id},
#                                      {season}, {episode}.
#   VIDEO_PROVIDER_N_MEDIA_TYPES       "movie", "tv", or "movie,tv". Optional —
#                                      defaults to whichever URLs you set.
#   VIDEO_PROVIDER_N_REPORTS_PROGRESS  Set only if the provider DOCUMENTS playback
#                                      progress events. Default off (nothing is
#                                      recorded from the provider; a position is
#                                      never invented).
#   VIDEO_PROVIDER_N_REPORTS_FAILURE   Set only if the provider DOCUMENTS a
#                                      reliable failure signal. Default off, which
#                                      also means automatic fallback stays off for
#                                      that slot — a blank-but-loaded player is
#                                      indistinguishable from a working one.
#
# There are deliberately NO api-key / token / auth variables here. Add one only if
# the provider's documentation actually requires it, keep it SERVER ONLY (never
# NEXT_PUBLIC_), and never put a secret in a URL template — an embed URL is
# visible to the browser by nature.
#
# A template must be an https:// URL or a same-origin /path. Anything else is
# rejected and reported in the picker instead of being loaded.
#
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_1_NAME=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_1_ENABLED=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_1_PRIORITY=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_1_MOVIE_URL=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_1_TV_URL=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_1_MEDIA_TYPES=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_1_REPORTS_PROGRESS=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_1_REPORTS_FAILURE=
#
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_2_NAME=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_2_ENABLED=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_2_PRIORITY=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_2_MOVIE_URL=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_2_TV_URL=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_2_MEDIA_TYPES=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_2_REPORTS_PROGRESS=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_2_REPORTS_FAILURE=
#
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_3_NAME=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_3_ENABLED=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_3_PRIORITY=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_3_MOVIE_URL=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_3_TV_URL=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_3_MEDIA_TYPES=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_3_REPORTS_PROGRESS=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_3_REPORTS_FAILURE=
#
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_4_NAME=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_4_ENABLED=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_4_PRIORITY=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_4_MOVIE_URL=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_4_TV_URL=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_4_MEDIA_TYPES=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_4_REPORTS_PROGRESS=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_4_REPORTS_FAILURE=
#
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_5_NAME=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_5_ENABLED=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_5_PRIORITY=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_5_MOVIE_URL=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_5_TV_URL=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_5_MEDIA_TYPES=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_5_REPORTS_PROGRESS=
# VIDEO_PROVIDER_5_REPORTS_FAILURE=

# ---- Supabase (Phase 2: auth + database) ----
# Create a project at https://supabase.com, then Project Settings → API.
# Leave ALL of these blank to keep the app in "local mode": no accounts, and
# watchlist/history/progress persist in the browser's localStorage (the V1
# behavior). Fill them in to enable accounts + server-side persistence.
#
# URL + anon key are sent to the browser (NEXT_PUBLIC_) BY DESIGN — the anon key
# is safe to expose because every table is guarded by row-level security.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
#
# Service-role key: SERVER ONLY. Bypasses row-level security — never prefix it
# with NEXT_PUBLIC_ and never reference it from a client component. Optional;
# only needed for privileged server tasks (e.g. metadata cache writes).
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---- Cache (Phase 2: optional) ----
# TMDB responses are already cached by Next.js's built-in fetch Data Cache in
# both modes, so no cache service is required. A Redis URL is the documented
# path for a SHARED cache across multiple server instances (scale-out); wiring a
# client for it is a follow-up. Leave blank for the built-in per-instance cache.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
